      "text": "The warning comes from `qbit.register` and `qbit.index`. These are back-references from a `Qubit` to the register that contains it. They were deprecated and are removed in Qiskit 1.0+, where they raise an `AttributeError`.\n\nThe supported way is to ask the circuit where the bit is located. `qc.find_bit(qbit)` returns a `BitLocations` object. Its `.registers` field is a list of `(register, index_in_register)` pairs.\n\nI also changed the loop header. Unpacking `qc.data` entries as `(instruction, qargs, cargs)` tuples is deprecated, and that also produces a warning. The loop now reads the qubits from the `CircuitInstruction` via its `.qubits` attribute.\n\n```python\nimport qiskit\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit, QuantumRegister\n\nqr = QuantumRegister(2, 'q')\nqc = QuantumCircuit(qr)\nqc.h(0)\nqc.cx(0, 1)\n\n# FIX: legacy tuple unpacking of CircuitInstruction is deprecated -> iterate instructions and use .qubits, because CircuitInstruction exposes qubits as an attribute\nfor instruction in qc.data:\n    qbit: qiskit.circuit.Qubit = instruction.qubits[0]\n    # FIX: Bit.register/Bit.index back-references are deprecated/removed -> use qc.find_bit(qbit).registers, because the circuit maps each bit to its (register, index) pairs\n    print(qc.find_bit(qbit).registers[0][0])\n    print(qc.find_bit(qbit).registers[0][1])\n```",
      "type": "text"
    }
